1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:04,980
In Baltimore, a container ship the length of three football fields passes under a bridge

2
00:00:04,980 --> 00:00:07,160
that has carried commuters since 1977.

3
00:00:07,940 --> 00:00:10,320
Three days later, it will destroy it.

4
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:15,160
March 26, 2024, 1 .24 in the morning.

5
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:17,600
At the base of the bridge,

6
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:20,940
three Maryland Transportation Authority police officers get a call.

7
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:22,800
A ship has lost its steering,

8
00:00:23,060 --> 00:00:24,640
and it is heading straight for them.

9
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:29,620
90 seconds later, they do not have a bridge left to hold traffic on.

10
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:32,760
Six construction workers were on that bridge,

11
00:00:33,020 --> 00:00:34,580
filling potholes in the middle of the night.

12
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:38,600
All six of them died. For two years,

13
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:41,480
the story was simple. A ship lost power.

14
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:44,600
A bridge fell down. It was a tragic accident.

15
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:48,360
Then, in the summer of 2026,

16
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:52,060
federal prosecutors stood up and said it wasn't just an accident at all.

17
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:55,360
You're watching black box breakdown.

18
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,140
Prosecutors say this wasn't just an accident.

19
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:03,680
Chapter 1. The bridge that was never supposed to be hit.

20
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:07,400
The Francis Scott Key Bridge opened on March 23,

21
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:10,900
1977. It existed for one reason.

22
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:13,320
Baltimore's harbor tunnel had run out of room.

23
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:16,420
Planners looked at building a second tunnel.

24
00:01:17,080 --> 00:01:19,840
It priced out higher than a four -lane bridge across the water,

25
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:21,640
so they built the bridge instead,

26
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:26,960
finishing construction in 1977 at a reported cost of around $141 million.

27
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:30,940
It became the final link in Baltimore's Beltway.

28
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:35,660
And it became the only route across the harbor legally open to trucks carrying hazardous

29
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:38,400
materials. Both tunnels banned them outright.

30
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:43,840
By 2023, more than 12 million vehicles a year were crossing it,

31
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:47,200
generating $56 million in tolls for the state of Maryland.

32
00:01:48,340 --> 00:01:53,060
That same year, the Port of Baltimore it fed became the number one port in the entire

33
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:55,400
United States for automobiles and farm machinery,

34
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:57,700
52 million tons of cargo,

35
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,500
worth roughly $80 billion.

36
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:06,220
That port supported around 15 ,000 jobs directly and close to 140

37
00:02:06,220 --> 00:02:08,800
,000 jobs in the wider economy around it.

38
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:13,520
All of it depended on ships being able to reach open water under a bridge that was never

39
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:14,620
supposed to be in their way.

40
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:19,020
The bridge itself was what engineers call fracture -critical.

41
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:23,120
It had no backup. If one key support failed,

42
00:02:23,580 --> 00:02:27,020
there was nothing built into the design to stop the whole structure from coming down

43
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:31,100
with it. It was protected by concrete piers called dolphins,

44
00:02:31,540 --> 00:02:34,540
wrapped in timber, built to 1970's specifications.

45
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:37,500
In 1980, a vessel struck one.

46
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:39,960
The fendering held and stopped it.

47
00:02:41,060 --> 00:02:43,600
That near -miss was treated as proof the system worked.

48
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:45,500
When the piers were repaired,

49
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:48,920
they were rebuilt to the exact same 1970's standard.

50
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:53,920
Nobody upgraded them. Here is why that mattered so much.

51
00:02:54,540 --> 00:02:59,700
On May 9th, 1980, a sudden squall knocked out the radar on a phosphate freighter

52
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:01,840
called the Summit Venture in Tampa Bay,

53
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:07,760
Florida. Blind, the freighter struck a support column of the old Sunshine Skyway

54
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:10,220
Bridge. 1 ,200 feet of it came down.

55
00:03:10,940 --> 00:03:16,040
35 people died. 26 of them on a Greyhound bus that plunged with six vehicles into the

56
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:21,160
bay below. That disaster is the real reason the shipping and engineering industries

57
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:26,540
finally wrote a formal standard in 1991 for testing whether a bridge could survive

58
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:28,140
being struck by a modern ship.

59
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:32,040
The Key Bridge was already 14 years old by then.

60
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:36,680
It was never required to be tested against a standard written to prevent the exact kind

61
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:38,540
of disaster that was about to happen to it anyway.

62
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:44,520
After the 2024 collapse, the National Transportation Safety Board ran the numbers on

63
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:47,600
the Key Bridge anyway. What they found was not close.

64
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:53,180
The bridge's calculated risk of collapse from a ship strike was nearly 30 times higher

65
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:55,100
than what today's engineers consider acceptable,

66
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:59,120
and the state of Maryland had never once run that calculation on its own bridge.

67
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:05,160
It gets worse. The same investigators identified 68 other American bridges

68
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:09,560
in 19 states built the same way before the same standard existed.

69
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:12,040
Most of them have never been assessed either.

70
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:17,640
Chapter 2. The Ship. The MV Dali.

71
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:24,040
947 feet long. Built in South Korea in 2014 at the Hyundai Heavy Industries

72
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:27,840
Yard in Olsan and delivered to its first owner in March of 2015.

73
00:04:29,120 --> 00:04:30,580
For its first year at sea,

74
00:04:31,060 --> 00:04:34,520
it belonged to a Greek shipping company under different management entirely.

75
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:36,960
That changed in October of 2016.

76
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:42,720
The ship was sold to a Singapore company called Grace Ocean and placed under the technical

77
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:44,520
management of another Singapore company,

78
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,620
Synergy Marine. It was re -flagged to Singapore and stayed under that same ownership

79
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:50,640
through the night of the collapse.

80
00:04:52,140 --> 00:04:53,800
The Dali did not belong to Mask,

81
00:04:54,280 --> 00:04:56,480
the shipping giant whose containers it was carrying.

82
00:04:57,220 --> 00:04:58,800
Mask was only the charterer,

83
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:04,120
a customer renting cargo space on someone else's ship with no role in operating it.

84
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:06,860
This was not the ship's first accident.

85
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:11,220
In July of 2016, while leaving port in Antwerp,

86
00:05:11,620 --> 00:05:13,180
Belgium, in good weather,

87
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:15,740
it struck a berth wall while unmooring.

88
00:05:16,140 --> 00:05:18,780
The stern was damaged. No one was hurt.

89
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:22,440
Nothing spilled. Investigators pointed to error on the bridge.

90
00:05:23,720 --> 00:05:28,940
By 2024, the Dali had been boarded for port inspections at least 27 times since

91
00:05:28,940 --> 00:05:32,540
entering service. Twice, inspectors found something wrong.

92
00:05:33,340 --> 00:05:35,020
Both times it was a minor deficiency,

93
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:36,660
fixed before the ship sailed.

94
00:05:37,140 --> 00:05:38,360
It was never once detained.

95
00:05:39,860 --> 00:05:41,300
The most recent deficiency,

96
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:44,720
found in June of 2023 at a port call in Chile,

97
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:47,340
was a faulty fuel pressure monitoring gauge.

98
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:50,400
It would not be the last time fuel pressure on this ship mattered.

99
00:05:51,420 --> 00:05:55,580
The US Coast Guard itself inspected the Dali in September of 2023.

100
00:05:56,220 --> 00:05:57,600
It found nothing wrong at all.

101
00:05:58,200 --> 00:06:02,740
Separately, Singapore's own maritime and port authority later confirmed the ship's statutory

102
00:06:02,740 --> 00:06:07,040
certificates were valid and that it had cooperated fully with the Coast Guard's investigation.

103
00:06:08,580 --> 00:06:13,860
On paper, this was a normal ship with a normal ownership structure that looked fine every

104
00:06:13,860 --> 00:06:15,440
single time anyone actually checked.

105
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:17,940
Just after 1 in the morning,

106
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:22,420
on March 26, 2024, the Dali left the port of Baltimore,

107
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:24,420
bound for Colombo, Sri Lanka,

108
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:29,520
a 27 -day voyage. Roughly 4 ,700 containers on board,

109
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:32,560
dozens of them carrying cargo classified as hazardous.

110
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:35,460
It never made it out of the harbour.

111
00:06:37,660 --> 00:06:42,840
Chapter 3, 4 minutes and 9 seconds Everything that happened

112
00:06:42,840 --> 00:06:45,400
next comes from the Dali's own voyage data recorder,

113
00:06:46,020 --> 00:06:47,540
the ship's version of a black box.

114
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:50,640
Federal investigators reconstructed it down to the second.

115
00:06:52,120 --> 00:06:57,520
1 .24 in the morning. The recorder shows the Dali holding a true heading of 141 degrees,

116
00:06:58,020 --> 00:07:00,720
moving at about 8 knots. Nothing unusual.

117
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:06,540
Then, at 1 .2459, the ship's electrical system automatically switches power

118
00:07:06,540 --> 00:07:08,440
sources, and the alarms start.

119
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:14,820
1 .25 in 0 seconds. The Dali is 6 tenths of a mile from the bridge when the lights go

120
00:07:14,820 --> 00:07:20,100
out completely. A single wire inside the ship's main electrical switchboard

121
00:07:20,100 --> 00:07:22,600
had come loose, not from damage,

122
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:25,060
from how it was installed in the first place.

123
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:28,400
The wire had a label wrapped around it,

124
00:07:28,760 --> 00:07:32,340
a heat -shrunk plastic sleeve identifying which circuit it belonged to.

125
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:35,220
That label was wrapped too far down the wire.

126
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:39,140
It covered the metal collar that was supposed to lock into the terminal block underneath

127
00:07:39,140 --> 00:07:41,780
it. It never fully seated,

128
00:07:42,520 --> 00:07:43,700
and for who knows how long,

129
00:07:43,960 --> 00:07:48,000
it sat there, vibrating loose a little more with every mile the ship sailed.

130
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:52,400
Investigators later noted that routine infrared inspection could have caught it.

131
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:54,580
Nobody had run one. That night,

132
00:07:54,920 --> 00:07:58,420
the wire finally let go. When it disconnected,

133
00:07:59,040 --> 00:08:01,180
a breaker tripped that it was never supposed to trip.

134
00:08:02,020 --> 00:08:05,900
Steering pumps died. The main engine shut down eight seconds later.

135
00:08:06,780 --> 00:08:08,300
Most of the ship's lights went dark.

136
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:10,920
Fifty -eight seconds later,

137
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:12,920
low -voltage power comes back on.

138
00:08:13,460 --> 00:08:14,920
The crew has restored some control.

139
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:16,960
But the ship is still coasting,

140
00:08:17,380 --> 00:08:19,520
dead in the water, drifting toward the bridge.

141
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:25,920
1 .25 59. The senior harbour pilot picks up the phone and calls the pilot's shoreside

142
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:27,900
dispatcher. Seven seconds.

143
00:08:28,540 --> 00:08:30,160
He says the ship has lost steering.

144
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:31,760
It's headed for the key bridge,

145
00:08:32,140 --> 00:08:33,540
and someone needs to close it.

146
00:08:33,760 --> 00:08:37,720
Right now. This call, not a radio broadcast,

147
00:08:38,240 --> 00:08:40,560
not an alarm, a seven -second phone call,

148
00:08:40,840 --> 00:08:44,580
is the single action that federal investigators would later credit with preventing an

149
00:08:44,580 --> 00:08:46,420
even greater loss of life that night.

150
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:51,060
1 .26 13. The pilot orders the rudder hard to port,

151
00:08:51,420 --> 00:08:53,380
trying to steer the dead ship away from the pier.

152
00:08:53,920 --> 00:08:56,820
1 .26 39. They call for a tugboat,

153
00:08:57,120 --> 00:08:59,540
the Eric McAllister. It's three miles away.

154
00:08:59,540 --> 00:09:01,720
It will not get there in time.

155
00:09:02,920 --> 00:09:07,080
1 .27 and two seconds. With the bridge less than two ship lengths away,

156
00:09:07,620 --> 00:09:09,380
the senior pilot gives one more order.

157
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:14,560
Drop the anchor. He would later tell investigators he knew it would not stop the ship.

158
00:09:15,340 --> 00:09:19,240
He hoped, at best, it might nudge the bow a few degrees off course.

159
00:09:19,740 --> 00:09:21,660
It was the only card he had left to play,

160
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:24,860
and he knew it. Two seconds later,

161
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:26,780
at 1 .27 and four seconds,

162
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:28,720
the ship loses power again.

163
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:32,660
A second blackout. A completely different cause than the first.

164
00:09:34,140 --> 00:09:38,320
Two of the ship's four generators were being fed fuel through a pump that was never designed

165
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:39,400
to run them continuously.

166
00:09:40,380 --> 00:09:41,720
When the fuel pressure dropped,

167
00:09:42,100 --> 00:09:47,060
the system disconnected both generators and blacked out the entire vessel a second time.

168
00:09:48,420 --> 00:09:52,060
Remember that detail. A fuel pump doing a job it wasn't built for.

169
00:09:52,420 --> 00:09:56,360
It will come up again. In a federal courtroom two years from now.

170
00:09:57,280 --> 00:10:01,640
1 .2711. The pilot orders a radio call to warn other ships in the water.

171
00:10:02,260 --> 00:10:05,460
At 1 .2725, it goes out over the marine radio.

172
00:10:05,780 --> 00:10:07,880
A security call. A safety warning.

173
00:10:08,320 --> 00:10:12,040
Not a distress signal. There was no Mayday broadcast before the bridge came down.

174
00:10:13,620 --> 00:10:16,820
1 .2736. Power comes back a second time.

175
00:10:17,620 --> 00:10:19,100
93 seconds before impact.

176
00:10:19,560 --> 00:10:23,820
At 1 .2746, the pilot orders the bow thrusted to full power,

177
00:10:24,020 --> 00:10:25,360
hoping it can still swing the bow.

178
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:29,640
It cannot. The ship is moving too fast for it to have any real effect.

179
00:10:31,380 --> 00:10:35,980
1 .2810. The bow finally drifts out of the shipping channel and begins grounding in the

180
00:10:35,980 --> 00:10:38,380
mud of the riverbed. It does not matter anymore.

181
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:40,680
The ship has no room left to recover.

182
00:10:42,700 --> 00:10:45,320
1 .2821. On the bridge itself,

183
00:10:46,100 --> 00:10:47,980
Maryland Transportation Authority police officers,

184
00:10:48,520 --> 00:10:51,660
already stationed at both ends that night for an unrelated road project,

185
00:10:51,940 --> 00:10:53,460
get the word and act immediately.

186
00:10:54,760 --> 00:10:56,560
Hold all traffic on the key bridge.

187
00:10:57,160 --> 00:10:59,420
There's a ship approaching that just lost their steering.

188
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:01,480
So until they get that under control,

189
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:02,960
we've got to stop all traffic.

190
00:11:03,560 --> 00:11:08,020
Real words from a real radio call at roughly 1 .27 that morning.

191
00:11:09,720 --> 00:11:13,180
1 .2822. Both ends of the bridge are closed to vehicles.

192
00:11:13,960 --> 00:11:15,340
48 seconds before impact.

193
00:11:16,860 --> 00:11:20,280
1 .2859. Ten seconds before the ship reaches the bridge,

194
00:11:20,760 --> 00:11:23,060
the anchor chain is finally heard running out on the recorder.

195
00:11:23,620 --> 00:11:25,100
It is far too late to matter.

196
00:11:26,380 --> 00:11:30,440
1 .29 and 9 seconds. The Dali's bow strikes pier 17.

197
00:11:32,020 --> 00:11:34,740
Six seconds later, span 18 begins to fall.

198
00:11:35,440 --> 00:11:36,920
Then the pier itself gives way.

199
00:11:37,500 --> 00:11:41,240
Then span 20. Then 21. Then 22.

200
00:11:42,020 --> 00:11:45,240
With a bridge inspector's own vehicle still sitting on top of it.

201
00:11:46,440 --> 00:11:49,660
Total time, from the very first blackout to the moment of impact.

202
00:11:50,220 --> 00:11:53,860
4 minutes and 9 seconds. Chapter 4.

203
00:11:54,480 --> 00:11:56,760
Reopening a harbour that was completely sealed shut.

204
00:11:58,140 --> 00:12:02,000
Within hours, the mission on the water changed from search and rescue to recovery.

205
00:12:02,580 --> 00:12:06,160
But there was a second, much bigger problem forming underneath the surface.

206
00:12:07,600 --> 00:12:11,760
Thousands of tons of steel truss now sat across the bottom of one of the busiest shipping

207
00:12:11,760 --> 00:12:12,900
channels on the east coast.

208
00:12:13,460 --> 00:12:16,780
The Port of Baltimore, the country's number one port for automobiles,

209
00:12:17,240 --> 00:12:22,380
was completely sealed. More than 50 salvage divers went into that water.

210
00:12:23,120 --> 00:12:26,200
Twelve cranes were brought in to start cutting the wreckage into pieces small enough

211
00:12:26,200 --> 00:12:30,620
to lift out. Six heavy lift crane barges worked the site,

212
00:12:30,940 --> 00:12:32,300
including the Chesapeake 1000,

213
00:12:32,940 --> 00:12:35,380
the largest crane barge on the entire east coast.

214
00:12:35,380 --> 00:12:39,960
Nearly 200 feet long, capable of lifting a thousand tons in a single pull.

215
00:12:41,460 --> 00:12:43,280
The first goal was never reopening the bridge.

216
00:12:43,920 --> 00:12:44,940
It was smaller than that.

217
00:12:45,280 --> 00:12:48,560
Just cutting one path back into the harbour wide enough for something,

218
00:12:48,980 --> 00:12:50,280
anything, to get through.

219
00:12:51,820 --> 00:12:56,420
On April 5th, 2024, President Biden flew over the wreckage by helicopter,

220
00:12:56,880 --> 00:12:59,260
then came down to meet the responders working it.

221
00:12:59,900 --> 00:13:01,180
Your nation has your back,

222
00:13:01,400 --> 00:13:04,780
he told them. By the end of that same month,

223
00:13:05,100 --> 00:13:08,140
engineers had cut a temporary channel 280 feet wide.

224
00:13:08,700 --> 00:13:12,160
By the end of May, almost exactly two months after the collapse,

225
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:14,880
the full, permanent shipping channel,

226
00:13:15,400 --> 00:13:17,700
700 feet wide and 50 feet deep,

227
00:13:17,720 --> 00:13:22,220
was open again. The port came back to life in about two months.

228
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:24,480
The bridge itself would take years.

229
00:13:24,480 --> 00:13:27,920
And the deeper question, not how to clear the water,

230
00:13:28,380 --> 00:13:30,300
but why any of this happened at all,

231
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:32,480
would take even longer than that.

232
00:13:33,840 --> 00:13:38,920
Chapter 5. The 48 Seconds That Didn't Reach Everyone The same

233
00:13:38,920 --> 00:13:43,260
fast thinking that stopped vehicle traffic 48 seconds before impact never reached everyone

234
00:13:43,260 --> 00:13:44,940
who was actually on the bridge that night.

235
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:49,320
A seven -person paving crew from Brawner Builders was out that night,

236
00:13:49,520 --> 00:13:51,980
filling potholes. An eighth man,

237
00:13:52,380 --> 00:13:55,000
a state bridge inspector working for a separate contractor,

238
00:13:55,020 --> 00:13:57,480
was walking the span doing his own inspection.

239
00:13:58,960 --> 00:14:00,360
They had their own radio channel,

240
00:14:00,940 --> 00:14:01,920
separate from the police,

241
00:14:02,460 --> 00:14:05,640
separate from the ship, separate from everything moving on the water below them.

242
00:14:05,960 --> 00:14:08,340
On that channel, in the minutes before the collapse,

243
00:14:08,720 --> 00:14:10,760
there was exactly one relevant exchange.

244
00:14:12,220 --> 00:14:14,200
One worker asked what was going on.

245
00:14:14,580 --> 00:14:18,380
Another answered, they're just holding traffic because a ship lost its steering,

246
00:14:18,620 --> 00:14:22,900
that's all. The bridge collapsed less than 30 seconds later.

247
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:25,620
No evacuation order ever went out on that channel.

248
00:14:27,520 --> 00:14:29,580
The federal investigation would later state it plainly.

249
00:14:30,160 --> 00:14:34,480
The lack of effective, immediate communication to the highway workers was itself a contributing

250
00:14:34,480 --> 00:14:36,320
cause of the six deaths that followed,

251
00:14:37,040 --> 00:14:39,260
alongside the bridge's own structural vulnerability.

252
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:45,280
Within hours, Governor Wes Moore stood in front of cameras and declared a state of emergency.

253
00:14:45,980 --> 00:14:50,160
We are thankful for the brave men and women who are carrying out efforts to rescue those

254
00:14:50,160 --> 00:14:52,980
involved, he said, and pray for everyone's safety.

255
00:14:54,460 --> 00:14:55,960
One man on the span that night,

256
00:14:56,420 --> 00:14:57,720
road inspector Damon Davis,

257
00:14:58,120 --> 00:14:59,940
was walking its length when the ship struck.

258
00:15:00,720 --> 00:15:04,100
He ran. He reached the nearest surviving section of deck,

259
00:15:04,140 --> 00:15:06,820
just as the rest of the bridge gave way beneath his own car.

260
00:15:07,980 --> 00:15:12,440
A crew member named Moises Diaz had been scheduled for that same overnight shift.

261
00:15:12,440 --> 00:15:15,960
A last minute change may have kept him off the section that fell.

262
00:15:16,380 --> 00:15:19,660
He has since called the six men who died people he saw as family.

263
00:15:21,040 --> 00:15:25,060
The six men who did not make it off in time were not just names on an incident report.

264
00:15:25,700 --> 00:15:27,640
They had lives, in this city,

265
00:15:27,960 --> 00:15:30,900
that the bridge's collapse cut off in the middle of an ordinary work night.

266
00:15:31,860 --> 00:15:35,420
Jose Minor Lopez, 37 years old,

267
00:15:35,580 --> 00:15:37,440
born in Camitán, Guatemala.

268
00:15:38,120 --> 00:15:39,840
He lived in Dundalk, Maryland,

269
00:15:40,240 --> 00:15:42,240
and left behind a wife and four children.

270
00:15:43,800 --> 00:15:45,460
Alejandro Hernandez Fuentes,

271
00:15:45,920 --> 00:15:49,400
35. From Mexico, living in Baltimore,

272
00:15:50,020 --> 00:15:52,320
married, with four young children of his own.

273
00:15:53,300 --> 00:15:55,360
Dorlean Roniel Castillo Cabrera,

274
00:15:55,860 --> 00:15:59,460
26. From Guatemala, also living in Dundalk.

275
00:16:01,060 --> 00:16:02,820
Mayna Yasea Suazo Sandoval,

276
00:16:03,260 --> 00:16:06,380
38. From Azacualpa, Honduras.

277
00:16:07,100 --> 00:16:09,680
He was a father of two, a teenage son,

278
00:16:10,060 --> 00:16:11,260
and a six -year -old daughter.

279
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:14,640
Carlos Daniel Hernandez Estrella,

280
00:16:15,100 --> 00:16:17,360
24 years old, born in Michoacán,

281
00:16:17,780 --> 00:16:21,000
Mexico. Miguel Angel Luna Gonzalez,

282
00:16:21,900 --> 00:16:25,500
49. From El Salvador, living in Glen Burney,

283
00:16:25,740 --> 00:16:27,580
Maryland, a father of six.

284
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:32,360
Four countries, six men. Between them,

285
00:16:32,480 --> 00:16:34,520
they left behind wives, husbands,

286
00:16:35,040 --> 00:16:36,260
and more than a dozen children.

287
00:16:37,720 --> 00:16:39,940
One crew member, Julio Cervantes,

288
00:16:40,360 --> 00:16:42,320
fell into the river and swam to shore.

289
00:16:42,700 --> 00:16:44,580
Despite reportedly not knowing how to swim,

290
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:47,760
he is one of only two men on that bridge who made it off alive.

291
00:16:48,860 --> 00:16:51,040
One year later, on the anniversary,

292
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:53,460
Governor Moore returned to the water's edge.

293
00:16:54,240 --> 00:16:55,420
We also remember the heroes,

294
00:16:55,660 --> 00:17:00,480
he said. In that moment, we showed what it means to be Maryland tough and Baltimore strong.

295
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:07,460
Chapter 6. What the Investigation Actually Found March 20,

296
00:17:07,840 --> 00:17:11,360
2025. Almost a year before the final report,

297
00:17:11,880 --> 00:17:15,920
NTSB chair Jennifer Homendy calls an urgent press conference of her own,

298
00:17:15,920 --> 00:17:18,900
because the risk was too serious to wait for.

299
00:17:20,080 --> 00:17:21,380
We need action, she said.

300
00:17:22,160 --> 00:17:23,540
Public safety depends on it.

301
00:17:23,820 --> 00:17:26,680
Her point, if Maryland had run the vulnerability assessment,

302
00:17:26,860 --> 00:17:28,700
it never ran, she said plainly.

303
00:17:29,180 --> 00:17:30,520
The collapse could have been prevented.

304
00:17:31,960 --> 00:17:33,960
Eight months later, on November 18,

305
00:17:34,400 --> 00:17:37,760
2025, the board released its full final report,

306
00:17:38,380 --> 00:17:43,700
259 pages. Homendy opened that meeting with a line that stuck with reporters

307
00:17:43,700 --> 00:17:45,920
in the room. The fact is,

308
00:17:46,040 --> 00:17:48,220
she said, we shouldn't be here today.

309
00:17:48,680 --> 00:17:50,320
This tragedy should have never occurred.

310
00:17:51,660 --> 00:17:55,940
She had her own way of explaining just how hard it was to find one loose wire on a ship

311
00:17:55,940 --> 00:17:59,380
that size. The Dali is almost a thousand feet,

312
00:17:59,860 --> 00:18:01,460
and it's as long as the Eiffel Tower,

313
00:18:01,660 --> 00:18:06,720
she said. Locating a single wire that is loose among thousands of wires is like looking

314
00:18:06,720 --> 00:18:08,480
for a loose bolt in the Eiffel Tower.

315
00:18:09,640 --> 00:18:11,840
She also asked, on the record,

316
00:18:12,380 --> 00:18:14,740
why? A year and a half after the collapse,

317
00:18:15,440 --> 00:18:19,160
Synergy still hadn't updated its own policies to tell its crews to stop relying on that

318
00:18:19,160 --> 00:18:22,920
backup fuel pump. The board's official conclusion,

319
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:28,140
in writing, the probable cause was a loss of electrical power due to a loose signal wire

320
00:18:28,140 --> 00:18:33,380
connection caused by improperly installed wire label banding that cost the Dali its

321
00:18:33,380 --> 00:18:35,400
propulsion and steering close to the bridge.

322
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:41,140
The board went further. It found that the lack of any real protection against a ship

323
00:18:41,140 --> 00:18:43,000
strike contributed to the bridge's collapse,

324
00:18:43,640 --> 00:18:47,620
and that the lack of fast communication to the road crew contributed to the loss of life.

325
00:18:49,060 --> 00:18:52,520
One mechanical fault, one design gap from decades earlier,

326
00:18:52,860 --> 00:18:54,900
one communication failure in the final minute,

327
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:58,380
three separate causes, all named in the same official finding.

328
00:18:59,320 --> 00:19:03,320
The physical damage, $18 million to the ship itself,

329
00:19:03,740 --> 00:19:07,620
between $4 .3 and $5 .2 billion to rebuild the bridge.

330
00:19:08,880 --> 00:19:11,940
The bridge is not expected to reopen until the end of 2030.

331
00:19:12,820 --> 00:19:17,120
More than 34 ,000 vehicles a day are still being permanently rerouted around a harbour

332
00:19:17,120 --> 00:19:18,620
that used to carry them straight across.

333
00:19:19,680 --> 00:19:24,000
And the 68 other American bridges the board flagged are similarly untested?

334
00:19:24,860 --> 00:19:28,020
As of this recording, most of them still haven't been assessed.

335
00:19:29,540 --> 00:19:31,360
That is the settled part of this story.

336
00:19:31,980 --> 00:19:33,880
The wire, the design gap,

337
00:19:34,240 --> 00:19:35,520
the communication failure.

338
00:19:35,980 --> 00:19:40,680
A federal agency spent 20 months proving exactly what went wrong and published every

339
00:19:40,680 --> 00:19:42,260
page of it for anyone to read.

340
00:19:43,300 --> 00:19:45,040
What that report could not settle,

341
00:19:45,740 --> 00:19:47,260
because it was never its job to settle,

342
00:19:47,920 --> 00:19:52,940
was who, if anyone, should be held criminally responsible for it.

343
00:19:53,700 --> 00:19:57,300
Chapter 7 The Case May 12,

344
00:19:57,800 --> 00:20:01,300
2026. Two years and seven weeks after the collapse,

345
00:20:01,980 --> 00:20:05,660
federal officials gather for a press conference aboard a ship docked in southeast Baltimore,

346
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,580
with the wreckage of the bridge visible in the water behind them.

347
00:20:09,480 --> 00:20:14,520
There, the United States Department of Justice unseals an 18 -count federal indictment,

348
00:20:16,060 --> 00:20:19,860
named Synergy Marine, the company that technically managed the Dali,

349
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:22,660
a related firm Synergy Maritime,

350
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:27,580
and one individual, a technical superintendent named Radhakrishnan Karthiknair.

351
00:20:28,660 --> 00:20:30,740
To be clear about what this is,

352
00:20:31,200 --> 00:20:34,020
an indictment is an accusation brought by prosecutors.

353
00:20:34,900 --> 00:20:36,480
Nothing in it has been proven in court.

354
00:20:37,180 --> 00:20:38,540
Mr. Nair has pleaded nothing,

355
00:20:38,900 --> 00:20:41,520
because he has never once appeared in an American courtroom.

356
00:20:43,140 --> 00:20:46,180
The charges, conspiracy to defraud the United States,

357
00:20:46,800 --> 00:20:48,940
causing the deaths of six construction workers,

358
00:20:49,500 --> 00:20:51,620
failing to warn the Coast Guard of a known hazard,

359
00:20:52,240 --> 00:20:53,740
obstructing the federal investigation,

360
00:20:54,400 --> 00:20:59,320
lying to investigators. The two Synergy companies face something else too,

361
00:21:00,320 --> 00:21:03,720
misdemeanor environmental charges for allegedly discharging containers,

362
00:21:04,220 --> 00:21:06,720
oil, and bridge debris into the Patapsco River.

363
00:21:07,580 --> 00:21:10,300
The Environmental Protection Agency's administrator put it bluntly,

364
00:21:10,980 --> 00:21:13,760
no foreign company should profit by poisoning American communities.

365
00:21:15,420 --> 00:21:18,140
At the center of the criminal case is that detail from earlier,

366
00:21:18,520 --> 00:21:22,700
the fuel pump. Prosecutors allege that since at least 2020,

367
00:21:23,180 --> 00:21:24,360
as a cost -cutting measure,

368
00:21:24,820 --> 00:21:26,060
Synergy had modified the Dali,

369
00:21:26,680 --> 00:21:31,160
and its sister ships, to feed two of its four generators through an unapproved flushing

370
00:21:31,160 --> 00:21:33,620
pump, instead of the proper fuel supply system.

371
00:21:34,700 --> 00:21:36,920
That pump, prosecutors say,

372
00:21:37,440 --> 00:21:40,000
could not automatically restart itself after a blackout.

373
00:21:40,900 --> 00:21:45,240
And that, they allege, is exactly why the second power loss turned fatal.

374
00:21:45,840 --> 00:21:48,880
The ship simply ran out of time to recover before it reached the bridge.

375
00:21:50,540 --> 00:21:52,200
Prosecutors further allege that Mr.

376
00:21:52,300 --> 00:21:56,080
Nair personally told federal investigators he didn't know the flushing pump was being

377
00:21:56,080 --> 00:21:59,080
used that way. They say that statement was false.

378
00:22:00,260 --> 00:22:05,340
And they allege that Synergy employees fabricated Coast Guard safety paperwork to keep

379
00:22:05,340 --> 00:22:09,920
the modification hidden from the one agency whose entire job was to catch it before it

380
00:22:09,920 --> 00:22:14,160
sailed. The indictment puts the total economic loss at,

381
00:22:14,560 --> 00:22:16,240
quote, at least $5 billion.

382
00:22:17,380 --> 00:22:18,920
Under federal fraud statutes,

383
00:22:19,300 --> 00:22:23,100
that number could theoretically expose the defendants to fines twice that size.

384
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:25,500
Nobody has been ordered to pay anything,

385
00:22:25,840 --> 00:22:28,940
not yet. As for Mr. Nair himself,

386
00:22:29,620 --> 00:22:30,880
he is believed to be in India.

387
00:22:31,440 --> 00:22:32,480
He has not been arrested.

388
00:22:32,940 --> 00:22:36,740
He is not out on bail, because he was never in custody to begin with.

389
00:22:37,440 --> 00:22:38,780
Prosecutors have said there is,

390
00:22:39,020 --> 00:22:41,720
quote, no concrete progress bringing him to Baltimore.

391
00:22:43,320 --> 00:22:48,300
By July of 2026, Synergy's own defense attorneys were asking the court to sever Nair

392
00:22:48,300 --> 00:22:53,080
from the trial entirely, arguing it's genuinely uncertain he will ever set foot in an

393
00:22:53,080 --> 00:22:54,500
American courtroom to face it.

394
00:22:55,340 --> 00:22:57,080
A month after the main indictment,

395
00:22:57,560 --> 00:23:00,860
in June 2026, a fourth man was charged separately,

396
00:23:01,420 --> 00:23:02,760
the Dali's chief engineer,

397
00:23:03,240 --> 00:23:05,300
Karthikeyan Dina Dailan, who,

398
00:23:05,600 --> 00:23:09,120
unlike Nair, had stayed in the United States since the night of the collapse.

399
00:23:10,680 --> 00:23:13,940
He was charged by a simplifying than a grand jury indictment,

400
00:23:14,520 --> 00:23:17,660
a legal signal that often points toward a negotiated resolution,

401
00:23:18,040 --> 00:23:22,520
not a fight in court. Synergy's own public response was sharp.

402
00:23:23,080 --> 00:23:26,720
It said DOJ was, quote, criminalising a tragic accident,

403
00:23:27,140 --> 00:23:31,140
arguing the charges are inconsistent with NTSB's own finding that the blackout made the

404
00:23:31,140 --> 00:23:33,300
outcome effectively unavoidable once it began.

405
00:23:33,880 --> 00:23:36,040
A senior Justice Department official called the collapse,

406
00:23:36,420 --> 00:23:39,020
quote, a preventable tragedy of enormous consequence.

407
00:23:40,640 --> 00:23:42,380
Governor Moore's own reaction was direct.

408
00:23:42,380 --> 00:23:44,820
We welcome today's indictments,

409
00:23:44,880 --> 00:23:49,900
he said, and remain committed to ensuring that all those responsible are held to account.

410
00:23:51,240 --> 00:23:55,620
If it goes to trial, that trial is scheduled to run from October through November of

411
00:23:55,620 --> 00:23:59,660
2027, three and a half years after the bridge came down.

412
00:24:00,860 --> 00:24:03,260
Chapter 8. Everyone else who paid for this.

413
00:24:04,180 --> 00:24:07,280
The criminal case is not the only track moving through the courts.

414
00:24:07,980 --> 00:24:10,560
On the very same day, the indictment was unsealed.

415
00:24:11,020 --> 00:24:13,340
Maryland's attorney general announced something else entirely.

416
00:24:14,800 --> 00:24:19,300
A $2 .25 billion civil settlement between the state of Maryland and the ship's owner

417
00:24:19,300 --> 00:24:24,300
and operator. More than 50 times the roughly $44 million those companies

418
00:24:24,300 --> 00:24:28,740
had originally tried to cap their total liability at under a maritime law dating back

419
00:24:28,740 --> 00:24:32,720
to 1851. That is a civil payment to the state.

420
00:24:33,200 --> 00:24:37,280
It is a completely separate track from the criminal charges and separate again from what

421
00:24:37,280 --> 00:24:38,600
happened with the victims' families.

422
00:24:40,220 --> 00:24:45,220
In late May of 2026, Synergy and Grace Ocean reached confidential settlements with the

423
00:24:45,220 --> 00:24:47,680
families of all six workers and with survivor,

424
00:24:47,680 --> 00:24:51,120
Julio Cervantes. The amounts were never made public.

425
00:24:52,160 --> 00:24:54,440
The family's attorney called the outcome,

426
00:24:54,920 --> 00:24:56,800
quote, bittersweet, because,

427
00:24:57,080 --> 00:25:01,040
he said, they won't have the opportunity to experience the seasons of life with their

428
00:25:01,040 --> 00:25:03,940
husbands, fathers, brothers and sons.

429
00:25:05,560 --> 00:25:10,060
Those same settlements caused a separate federal civil trial over the city of Baltimore's

430
00:25:10,060 --> 00:25:14,140
own economic losses to be paused just days before it was set to begin.

431
00:25:14,140 --> 00:25:18,800
Some of those remaining claims may run into a nearly 100 -year -old Supreme Court precedent

432
00:25:18,800 --> 00:25:21,960
that limits who can sue for pure economic loss at sea.

433
00:25:22,460 --> 00:25:23,720
It still has not been resolved.

434
00:25:25,180 --> 00:25:28,760
But it was never just the state or the city that paid for this.

435
00:25:29,520 --> 00:25:33,860
For two years, the loss of that bridge reached into ordinary businesses that had nothing

436
00:25:33,860 --> 00:25:38,780
to do with any of it. Bulls Trucking Company watched its volume drop from as many as

437
00:25:38,780 --> 00:25:41,320
4 ,000 trucks a day to zero overnight.

438
00:25:42,040 --> 00:25:44,980
Other haulers rerouted through the harbor tunnels or around the Beltway.

439
00:25:45,540 --> 00:25:49,120
The American Trucking Association's estimated 4 ,900 trucks a day,

440
00:25:49,420 --> 00:25:51,760
carrying $28 billion a year in goods,

441
00:25:52,120 --> 00:25:53,520
suddenly needed a different way through.

442
00:25:54,860 --> 00:25:56,820
One local trucker, Robert Lovett,

443
00:25:57,240 --> 00:26:01,440
ended up taking a $25 ,000 federal disaster loan just to keep his company running.

444
00:26:02,580 --> 00:26:05,400
Down on the water, the crab houses felt it too.

445
00:26:06,260 --> 00:26:07,500
We're not getting enough crabs,

446
00:26:07,720 --> 00:26:10,260
said Stacey Guzman, who owns Chris Seafood,

447
00:26:10,540 --> 00:26:13,940
as golf prices spiked $34 a box that same week.

448
00:26:15,560 --> 00:26:19,560
Costas Inn, a crab house that has been open since the bridge was first being built in

449
00:26:19,560 --> 00:26:24,000
the 1970s, a place that once fed the very steelworkers who put the bridge up,

450
00:26:24,300 --> 00:26:27,260
stayed open anyway and turned down a disaster loan.

451
00:26:28,220 --> 00:26:30,960
The people who built it, and the people who fed,

452
00:26:31,280 --> 00:26:33,960
the people who built it, both outlasted the bridge itself.

453
00:26:35,580 --> 00:26:39,180
The replacement being built now is Maryland's first cable -stayed highway bridge,

454
00:26:39,620 --> 00:26:41,480
two towers over 600 feet tall,

455
00:26:41,860 --> 00:26:44,660
a main span held up by 144 steel cables.

456
00:26:46,120 --> 00:26:50,960
This time, its piers are being driven as 276 concrete -filled steel columns,

457
00:26:51,380 --> 00:26:55,360
eight feet across, built to the modern vessel collision standard the original bridge

458
00:26:55,360 --> 00:26:59,940
never had to meet. But even this project has not gone smoothly.

459
00:27:00,940 --> 00:27:05,640
In April of 2026, the state terminated its main contractors agreement for the bridge's

460
00:27:05,640 --> 00:27:10,720
second phase, after its cost proposal blew past the state's own $5 billion estimate.

461
00:27:11,680 --> 00:27:13,060
As of the most recent reporting,

462
00:27:13,540 --> 00:27:16,300
no replacement contractor for that phase has been publicly named.

463
00:27:17,500 --> 00:27:18,680
Current target for reopening,

464
00:27:19,140 --> 00:27:21,960
the end of 2030. On the two -year anniversary,

465
00:27:22,540 --> 00:27:23,620
Governor Moore said it plainly.

466
00:27:24,420 --> 00:27:28,240
I plan on being the governor who cut the ribbon to reopen the Francis Scott Key Bridge.

467
00:27:29,720 --> 00:27:32,680
The wire that started all of this probably cost a few dollars.

468
00:27:33,220 --> 00:27:35,760
The plastic label wrapped around it cost even less.

469
00:27:37,160 --> 00:27:38,700
The bridge won't reopen for years.

470
00:27:39,120 --> 00:27:40,600
The criminal trial hasn't started.

471
00:27:41,320 --> 00:27:45,020
Federal investigators have gone as far as asking the entire global shipping industry

472
00:27:45,020 --> 00:27:47,760
to rewrite its own safety rulebook because of what happened here.

473
00:27:47,760 --> 00:27:50,340
A recommendation, not yet an answer.

474
00:27:51,840 --> 00:27:56,260
And 68 other bridges across 19 states are still sitting there,

475
00:27:56,540 --> 00:28:00,420
unassessed, exactly the way the Key Bridge was on March 25th,

476
00:28:00,420 --> 00:28:05,260
2024. Somewhere tonight, on one of those 68 bridges,

477
00:28:05,880 --> 00:28:07,020
cars are crossing over water,

478
00:28:07,400 --> 00:28:11,360
on a structure nobody has ever actually tested against a ship exactly like the Dali.

479
00:28:12,100 --> 00:28:15,480
That is not a theory. That is the federal government's own list.

480
00:28:17,020 --> 00:28:20,580
If a single wire wrapped the wrong way brought down an American bridge,

481
00:28:21,120 --> 00:28:25,040
wait until you see what a single mislabeled light did to a nuclear reactor control room

482
00:28:25,040 --> 00:28:28,780
in Pennsylvania. And if you want more real investigations like this one,

483
00:28:29,360 --> 00:28:30,540
subscribe to Black Box Breakdown.

484
00:28:31,600 --> 00:28:33,220
If this one got under your skin a little,

485
00:28:33,620 --> 00:28:38,640
good. That's the point. And it only keeps happening because people like you keep showing

486
00:28:38,640 --> 00:28:40,760
up. So do one thing for me,

